ELK Garden — Architecture Notes (Block 1)

Decisions locked in
	•	Use Prisma + SQLite for V1 foundation
	•	Treat the current garden structure as the editable draft
	•	Store generated output separately in GeneratedPlan
	•	Do not create a separate Crop table yet; keep cropName as a string on Row

⸻

Future auth compatibility

Yes — this structure supports authentication later.

Why

Even if V1 starts with a default or fake user, the model already assumes ownership:
	•	User owns many Garden records
	•	Garden owns Area, Row, Task, and GeneratedPlan data indirectly

That means when real auth is added later, we do not need to redesign the whole data model.
We mainly need to:
	•	replace the fake/default user flow with a real authenticated user
	•	attach the signed-in user ID to created/read/updated records
	•	protect API routes so users only access their own gardens

Auth-ready philosophy

Build the data model as if auth already exists, even if implementation comes later.

⸻

ID strategy

Use UUID-style string IDs from the start.

Why not simple integer IDs?

Integers are fine technically, but UUID-style IDs are better for future product growth:
	•	easier to merge local/dev/test data safely
	•	better for multi-user systems
	•	safer to expose in URLs and APIs
	•	avoids simple record guessing like /gardens/1, /gardens/2
	•	closer to what many real SaaS apps use

Practical choice in Prisma

Use string IDs with a generated UUID/cuid.
For example:
	•	String @id @default(cuid())

This gives us stable unique IDs without needing Postgres-specific functions.

